## Task

Drive the plan in `$ARGUMENTS` through `csdd plan run`.

Preflight first: `csdd plan status <slug>` must show the plan **approved** and
**drift-free** (no unprocessed deviations you haven't reviewed). Then:

- `csdd plan run <slug>` — the runner spawns a fresh session per step with
  `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, runs the gates, and approves each phase only on
  green. Run it inside the hardened sandbox: the runner checks `csdd sandbox
  doctor` first, and when isolation is NOT verified it alerts and asks for
  confirmation — declining closes the run.
- `--yes` pre-accepts that alert (non-interactive runs); only pass it when the
  human has explicitly accepted running bypass-mode outside a verified sandbox.

The runner plays the human at each gate: it owns the phase approvals, while the
session runs the csdd dev-cycle and owns git (branch, `/csdd-commit`, the pre-push
gate, the PR) — the runner never commits.

## When the run stops with blocked feats

`csdd plan status <slug>` prints each block's **kind**, and the kind decides what to
do. Never `rm` a marker under `.csdd/` by hand.

- **`gate-failure` / `scaffold` / `brief`** — mechanical. The runner already
  retried, already spent a repair session on the full failure history, and wrote
  every attempt's output to `.csdd/plan/<slug>/failures/<feat>/<step>.log`. **Read
  that log before doing anything else.** If the fix is within the step's contract,
  make it, then `csdd plan unblock <slug> <feat>` and re-run. A feat that has spent
  its `--max-repairs` budget will not retry itself again until you do.
- **`deviation`** — the session hit an open decision, so the plan itself is wrong
  and no retry can answer it. Do not patch the plan mid-run and do not pass
  `--force`: take the recorded revision proposal to the `prd` skill's Revise
  workflow. Re-approving the revised plan (`csdd plan approve <slug>`) is what
  clears the block — re-approving an unchanged plan clears nothing.
